=== L1 Food Spoilage, Storage & Preservation ===
id: 1c5d5bf4-3cb2-4450-b593-1b0c3ed579ed
slug: food-spoilage-storage-preservation
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:24:19.195034+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Correct storage, date marks, signs of food spoilage, and five preservation methods from jam-making to vacuum packing.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Food Spoils</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every time you leave a bag of salad too long in the fridge or forget about a loaf of bread on the counter, you are watching food spoilage in action. <dfn class="term" data-def="The process by which food deteriorates to the point where it is no longer safe or suitable to eat, caused by micro-organisms, enzymes or chemical reactions.">Food spoilage</dfn> happens because of four main agents: enzyme action, mould growth, yeast activity and bacterial growth. Understanding why each agent acts &mdash; and how to slow it down &mdash; is central to safe food storage and food waste reduction.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Enzymes are biological catalysts naturally present in all plant and animal tissue. After a fruit or vegetable is harvested, its own enzymes continue to work, breaking down cell walls, changing colour and softening texture. A sliced apple turning brown is classic <dfn class="term" data-def="A chemical reaction in which enzymes in cut fruit or vegetables react with oxygen to produce brown pigments, causing discolouration.">enzymic browning</dfn>: the enzyme polyphenol oxidase reacts with oxygen in the air, turning the cut surface brown. Chilling, acidifying with lemon juice, or blanching in boiling water all inhibit the enzyme and slow browning.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Mould and Yeast</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><dfn class="term" data-def="Microscopic fungi that grow in branching thread-like structures called hyphae, producing visible fluffy growth and sometimes toxins on the surface of food.">Mould</dfn> needs moisture, warmth, oxygen and nutrients to colonise food. The blue-green fuzz on a forgotten piece of bread or the white patches on soft fruit are visible mould colonies. Mould grows well between 5&deg;C and 40&deg;C and produces mycotoxins that can be harmful even after the mouldy surface is cut away &mdash; particularly in soft foods where toxins penetrate deeply. Refrigerating food and keeping surfaces dry and clean are the most effective controls.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="A single-celled fungus that reproduces by budding and ferments sugars into carbon dioxide and alcohol.">Yeast</dfn> ferments sugars into carbon dioxide and alcohol. This is deliberately exploited in bread-making and brewing, but unwanted yeast growth causes fizzing and off-flavours in fruit juices and jams when conditions are warm and sugary. Reducing water activity &mdash; by adding large quantities of sugar, as in jam-making &mdash; prevents yeast from accessing the water it needs to multiply.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the four spoilage agents and one condition each requires to grow or act. Then name one storage method that controls each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four agents of food spoilage are enzyme action, mould growth, yeast activity and bacterial growth. All four need moisture and an appropriate temperature to be active. Removing or reducing one key condition &mdash; usually temperature, moisture or oxygen &mdash; is the basis of every preservation method.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Storing Food Correctly</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Correct storage slows the activity of all four spoilage agents. The key principles are: keep chilled foods below 5&deg;C, keep frozen foods at &minus;18&deg;C or below, keep dry goods in airtight containers away from moisture and heat, and cover or package food to prevent cross-contamination and exposure to air.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">A domestic fridge should run at 0&ndash;5&deg;C. Raw meat and fish should be stored on the bottom shelf, in a sealed container, below cooked foods and dairy. This prevents raw meat juices dripping onto foods eaten without further cooking. Fridges should not be overpacked, as air needs to circulate to maintain even temperature. A chest or upright freezer at &minus;18&deg;C puts bacterial activity into a near-complete stop: micro-organisms do not die but they cannot reproduce or produce toxins.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: &lsquo;Use By&rsquo; vs &lsquo;Best Before&rsquo; &mdash; they mean the same thing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Many people use these two terms interchangeably &mdash; but they have very different legal meanings. A <strong>Use By</strong> date appears on high-risk perishable foods such as raw meat, ready-to-eat meals and soft cheese. Eating food after its Use By date is a food-safety risk, even if the food looks and smells normal &mdash; dangerous levels of bacteria can be present without any visible signs. You must not sell or use food after this date.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">A <strong>Best Before</strong> date appears on lower-risk foods: dried pasta, tinned goods, frozen vegetables. After this date the food may lose quality (flavour, texture, colour) but it is generally still safe to eat. Understanding this distinction reduces unnecessary food waste. The UK Food Standards Agency is clear that Use By is a safety cut-off; Best Before is a quality guide only.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Food Labelling Requirements</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">UK food labels must legally carry: the product name, a list of ingredients in descending order of weight, allergen information (highlighted in bold), net quantity, a date mark (Use By or Best Before), storage conditions, name and address of the manufacturer, and country of origin for certain foods. Nutritional information per 100g is also required on most pre-packaged foods.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Storage instructions are crucial: a label reading &ldquo;once opened, refrigerate and use within 3 days&rdquo; is a food-safety instruction, not merely a suggestion. Students who understand labelling can make better purchasing decisions and reduce household food waste.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close your notes and write down three pieces of information that must legally appear on a food label. Then add a fourth from memory.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Use By = safety date (do not eat after). Best Before = quality date (safe to eat but may be lower quality). Both are controlled by UK food labelling law. Raw meat, fish and ready-to-eat foods carry Use By; dried, canned and frozen goods carry Best Before.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Signs of Food Spoilage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">You can identify spoilage through your senses &mdash; but remember that with bacterial contamination the food may appear, smell and taste perfectly normal while still being unsafe. Visible signs of spoilage include: mould growth (fuzzy colonies, discolouration), sliminess or sticky surfaces (bacterial biofilm), fizzing in liquids (yeast fermentation), browning and soft patches in fruit and vegetables (enzyme action), and curdling, souring or off-odours in dairy products. Meat that has turned grey-brown and developed an ammonia or sour smell has undergone bacterial and enzymic breakdown and should not be eaten.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Preservation Methods</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">Preservation extends the shelf life of food by disrupting the conditions that spoilage agents need. The main methods, and how each works, are set out below:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Freezing</strong> stores food at &minus;18&deg;C or below. Micro-organisms cannot reproduce at this temperature and enzyme activity slows dramatically. Frozen peas, fish fillets and leftover cooked mince can be safely stored for weeks or months. Food must be fully thawed before cooking &mdash; partially frozen poultry in a roasting tin will not reach a safe core temperature throughout.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Jam-making</strong> relies on high sugar concentration. When a jam reaches a sugar content of around 60&ndash;65%, the <dfn class="term" data-def="The amount of water available in food for micro-organism activity; expressed as a scale from 0 to 1. Reducing water activity inhibits bacterial and yeast growth.">water activity</dfn> drops so low that bacteria and yeast cannot access the water they need to grow. Combined with the high boiling temperature during cooking (which destroys micro-organisms), and airtight sealing of sterilised jars, jam can last up to two years unopened.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Pickling</strong> uses an acid environment, typically white or malt vinegar (pH around 2&ndash;3). Most bacteria, moulds and yeasts cannot survive in a strongly acidic medium. Pickled onions, pickled cabbage (sauerkraut) and pickled gherkins all exploit this principle. Some pickles combine acid with salt, further reducing water activity.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n24"><strong>Bottling (heat processing)</strong> involves packing food into sterilised jars and then heating them in a water bath so that the internal temperature reaches a point that destroys micro-organisms. The vacuum seal formed on cooling prevents recontamination. Bottled tomatoes and preserved fruit are common examples.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Vacuum packing</strong> removes oxygen from the packaging. Without oxygen, aerobic bacteria and moulds cannot grow, and oxidation is slowed. Vacuum-packed bacon, smoked salmon and ground coffee all benefit from this method. However, vacuum packing does not prevent anaerobic bacteria such as <em>Clostridium botulinum</em> (botulism) from growing in low-acid, low-temperature foods, so vacuum-packed products still require refrigeration and observance of Use By dates.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Comparing preservation methods: which is best for a home cook?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n26">Each method has different strengths. Freezing is highly versatile and preserves nutritional value well, but requires energy to maintain. Jam-making and pickling are low-tech and shelf-stable, but change flavour and texture significantly. Bottling requires precise temperature control and sterilisation technique. Vacuum packing needs specialist equipment unavailable in most home kitchens. A home cook wanting to preserve a glut of summer strawberries would likely choose jam-making or freezing; a commercial food producer packaging cooked meats would choose vacuum packing. The best method always depends on the food type, the storage environment and the intended shelf life.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n28">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Food spoilage is caused by four agents &mdash; enzyme action, mould growth, yeast activity and bacterial growth &mdash; all requiring moisture, warmth and nutrients. Correct storage (fridge below 5&deg;C, freezer at &minus;18&deg;C, airtight dry storage) controls each agent by removing one or more of the conditions it needs.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">Use By is a safety date on perishable high-risk foods; Best Before is a quality date on lower-risk shelf-stable foods. Eating food after a Use By date can be dangerous even when the food looks and smells normal.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31">Preservation methods &mdash; jam-making, pickling, freezing, bottling and vacuum packing &mdash; extend shelf life by disrupting the conditions that micro-organisms and enzymes need. Each method works via a different scientific principle: high sugar concentration, acidity, low temperature, heat destruction or oxygen removal.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n27">The command word <strong>Explain</strong> means &ldquo;give reasons for and/or causes of&rdquo; &mdash; use linking words such as &lsquo;because&rsquo;, &lsquo;this means that&rsquo; and &lsquo;as a result&rsquo; to connect each point to its effect. On a 6-mark Explain question about preservation, a common mistake is to <em>list</em> six preservation methods without developing any of them. You will not reach the top band this way. Instead, aim for two or three well-developed explanations: name the method, describe the scientific principle behind it (e.g. reducing water activity, removing oxygen, applying acid), and link it to a specific food example. Two fully developed chains with accurate food-science vocabulary will score higher than six bare names.</p>

