  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): Two or more food-poisoning bacteria identified (e.g. E. coli, Salmonella) with their sources and likely symptoms. Type of cross-contamination named (indirect) and explained. Conditions that could worsen the risk considered (e.g. time in danger zone, moisture in the meat, room temperature). Consequences for the diners and the canteen discussed (illness, financial, regulatory). Food-science vocabulary used accurately throughout: danger zone, cross-contamination, water activity, incubation period, core temperature. Multiple perspectives analysed — not just a list.\n\nSecure (5–6 marks): One or two bacteria named with source and symptoms. Cross-contamination route identified. Some conditions discussed. Consequences mentioned. Mostly accurate but one perspective underdeveloped or vocabulary partially imprecise.\n\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Cross-contamination identified as a risk. May name one bacterium. Limited analysis of conditions or consequences. Mainly descriptive rather than analytical.\n\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic identification of a hygiene risk. Little or no analysis. Vocabulary absent or inaccurate.\n\nAcceptable points include:\n- E. coli O157:H7 lives in the gut of cattle and is found in minced beef; can transfer via indirect cross-contamination (board) to ready-to-eat bread.\n- Salmonella may also be present in raw beef.\n- Indirect cross-contamination: bacteria move from the board surface to the bread without further cooking to destroy them.\n- Conditions: if the board was at room temperature and the mince was handled for any period in the 5–63°C danger zone, bacterial numbers could be sufficient to cause illness.\n- Consequences for diners: bloody diarrhoea, severe abdominal cramps, kidney complications (E. coli O157:H7 is particularly serious for vulnerable groups).\n- Consequences for the canteen: closure by environmental health officers, fines, civil claims, reputational damage."
 },
