  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): Two or three specific dishes or techniques named and explained with accurate food-science vocabulary. All three pillars of the cuisine definition addressed (ingredients, methods, presentation). Links made between technique and result (e.g. risotto stirring releases starch through gelatinisation to achieve creaminess; tempura batter is kept cold to minimise gluten development and produce a light, crisp coating). Cause-and-effect chains present throughout.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Two features present; most have a named dish and some food-science explanation. Development present but not consistently linked to the spec's three-part definition.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): Dishes named but explanation is descriptive (tastes nice, is popular) rather than scientific or structural. Little or no food-science vocabulary.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): One dish named with a very basic description.\n\nAcceptable Italian examples:\n- Risotto: Arborio rice contains high starch content; constant stirring releases starch granules through gelatinisation as they absorb warm stock, producing the characteristic creamy, fluid consistency. Stopping stirring or adding cold stock inhibits starch release.\n- Fresh pasta: kneading develops gluten in the flour-and-egg dough (gluten formation), giving elasticity and the slightly chewy bite of al dente pasta. Over-kneading toughens the gluten network; under-kneading produces a crumbly dough.\n- Slow-simmered ragù: long, low heat denatures the collagen in cheaper cuts of beef or pork, converting it to gelatine, which thickens the sauce and gives it body.\n\nAcceptable Japanese examples:\n- Tempura batter is made with ice-cold water and only lightly mixed to minimise gluten formation — less gluten means a lighter, more delicate crust that does not mask the flavour of the ingredient beneath.\n- Dashi stock (kombu + bonito) provides umami through glutamates and inosinates; this is the savoury taste receptor response distinct from sweet, sour, salty and bitter.\n- Maillard browning occurs on yakitori (charcoal-grilled chicken) and teriyaki — high-heat grilling causes proteins and reducing sugars on the surface to react, developing complex caramel-savoury flavour compounds."
