=== L5 Food Manufacturing, Processing & Modified Foods ===
id: f773cf84-620b-45a4-89f1-d03bf2367a2b
slug: food-manufacturing-processing-modified-foods
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:57:07.476289+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Primary and secondary processing, sensory and nutritional effects, fortification, and the role of food additives.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From Field to Factory: Primary Processing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="The first stage of food production, converting a raw agricultural material into a clean, safe, storable commodity — for example, milling wheat into flour or pasteurising milk.">Primary processing</dfn> is the first step that transforms a raw agricultural product into something safe, storable and ready to use as an ingredient. It takes place close to the point of origin — the farm, orchard or fishing boat — and usually involves transporting, cleaning and sorting the raw food before any cooking or combining takes place. When a wheat harvest arrives at a flour mill, the grain is first checked for foreign objects, washed and graded by size. Only once it has been cleaned and sorted does it move through the rollers that crack and separate bran from endosperm — producing the white, wholemeal or strong bread flours we buy in a supermarket.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Other everyday examples of primary processing include: washing and grading vegetables before bagging; separating cream from raw milk; filleting and freezing fish at sea; and husking, milling and polishing rice. At this stage, the food is still recognisable as coming from one raw material — it has not yet been mixed or cooked into something new.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, give two examples of primary processing and two examples of secondary processing. Make sure each example names the raw material AND the product.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Primary processing converts a raw food at its point of origin (e.g. milling wheat into flour, pasteurising milk). Secondary processing then uses primary products as ingredients to make a new, more complex food product (e.g. flour + yeast + water baked into bread; milk cultured and pressed into cheese).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Secondary Processing: Creating New Products</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="The stage where primary food products are used as ingredients to make a more complex food — for example, wheat flour baked into bread, or milk fermented and pressed into cheese.">Secondary processing</dfn> uses primary products as ingredients and combines or transforms them to create an entirely new food. The spec highlights three key pathways students must know.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Wheat to bread.</strong> Milled flour (primary processed) is mixed with water, yeast, salt and fat. The yeast ferments sugars in the dough, releasing carbon dioxide that makes the bread rise. Baking in a hot oven sets the gluten network and gives the loaf its firm crust. The resulting product — bread — is structurally and nutritionally different from plain flour: it has been leavened, partially cooked (improving digestibility) and its flavour has been developed through Maillard browning of the crust.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Milk to cheese and yoghurt.</strong> Pasteurised milk (primary processed) is the starting point. For yoghurt, live bacterial cultures (<em>Lactobacillus bulgaricus</em> and <em>Streptococcus thermophilus</em>) are added. They ferment lactose into lactic acid, lowering the pH and causing the milk proteins to coagulate into the thick, creamy gel we recognise. For hard cheese, a coagulant (rennet) is added to set the curd; the curd is then cut, pressed and matured. Both processes fundamentally alter the original milk&rsquo;s texture, flavour and nutritional profile.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Fruit to jams, jellies and juices.</strong> Fresh fruit (primary commodity) is heated with sugar to make jam — pectin from the fruit cell walls dissolves and re-sets on cooling to form a gel. Juice is extracted by pressing or centrifuging fruit pulp. These secondary processes extend shelf life dramatically: fresh strawberries last a few days; strawberry jam lasts a year or more because the high sugar concentration and heat treatment together inhibit microbial growth.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: primary vs secondary processing &mdash; a common mix-up</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A common error is to call any factory process &ldquo;primary processing&rdquo;. The rule is straightforward: primary processing gives you one clean ingredient still derived from one raw source (flour, milk, filleted fish). Secondary processing combines or fundamentally transforms primary ingredients into a new product (bread, cheese, fish fingers). If the question asks you to trace the journey of a tin of baked beans, the tomatoes are primary-processed (cleaned and sorted), the haricot beans are primary-processed (dried and sorted), but the finished tin of beans-in-tomato-sauce is secondary-processed because it combines multiple primary ingredients and is cooked before sealing.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">How Processing Affects Sensory and Nutritional Properties</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Processing nearly always changes food. Sometimes the changes are desirable; sometimes they represent a nutritional trade-off. Take <dfn class="term" data-def="Meat products treated with salt, nitrates or smoke to preserve them and develop distinctive flavour and colour &mdash; for example, bacon, salami and chorizo.">cured meat products</dfn> like bacon or salami: salt and nitrates are added to inhibit the growth of <em>Clostridium botulinum</em> and other spoilage bacteria, dramatically extending shelf life. The curing process develops a distinctive salty, smoky or spiced flavour that many people enjoy &mdash; a clear sensory benefit. However, the sodium content increases substantially, which is a nutritional concern for people managing blood pressure.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Boiling vegetables is another example where the nutritional cost is visible. Water-soluble vitamins (particularly vitamin C and B vitamins) leach into the cooking water and are lost if that water is discarded. Steaming the same vegetables preserves far more of the water-soluble vitamins because the food does not sit in water. Colour changes also occur: green vegetables like broccoli brighten briefly at around 60&ndash;70&deg;C as trapped air escapes from intercellular spaces, but prolonged boiling beyond 7 minutes breaks down the chlorophyll molecule, turning them an unappetising khaki.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Fortification and Modified Foods</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15"><dfn class="term" data-def="Adding nutrients to a food that were not originally present, or adding them back after processing has reduced natural levels &mdash; for example, adding vitamin D to breakfast cereal or folic acid to bread flour.">Fortification</dfn> is a technological development used to improve public health through food. In the UK, white and brown bread flour must by law be fortified with calcium, iron, thiamin (B1) and niacin (B3) &mdash; nutrients lost when the bran and germ are removed during milling. Similarly, most breakfast cereals are fortified with a range of vitamins and minerals. Fortification is widely viewed as a positive development: it tackles nutritional deficiencies at population scale without requiring any change in consumers&rsquo; behaviour.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><dfn class="term" data-def="Foods that have been changed at a biological or chemical level to alter their properties &mdash; for example, genetically modified (GM) crops, or reduced-fat spreads produced by restructuring fat molecules.">Modified foods</dfn> go further than fortification. Genetic modification (GM) involves altering the DNA of a crop plant to give it new properties: drought resistance, higher yield, or resistance to a specific pest. Enzymatic modification restructures molecules &mdash; for example, lactase-treated milk breaks down lactose so that lactose-intolerant people can drink it. Modified-atmosphere packaging replaces the air in a food packet with a mix of carbon dioxide and nitrogen to slow bacterial growth and extend shelf life without additives.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Positive and negative effects of food modification</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Positive effects include: higher crop yields that could help address food security; reduced pesticide use (GM pest-resistant crops); extended shelf life reducing food waste; and products accessible to people with intolerances (lactose-free milk). Negative concerns include: unknown long-term health effects; loss of biodiversity if GM monocultures dominate; ethical concerns about corporate patent control over seeds; and consumer distrust, as surveys consistently show many shoppers are reluctant to buy GM-labelled foods. A well-balanced exam answer acknowledges both sides rather than treating modification as wholly good or wholly bad.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Food Additives: Their Purpose and Function</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Additives are substances added to food during processing to perform a specific technical function. In the UK, all approved additives are assigned an <dfn class="term" data-def="A code number prefixed with E, assigned by European and UK food safety authorities to an approved additive &mdash; for example, E300 (vitamin C / ascorbic acid) as an antioxidant.">E number</dfn>, which means a safety assessment has been passed. The five main categories students need to know are:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Preservatives</strong> extend shelf life by slowing or stopping microbial growth. Sodium benzoate (E211) is used in soft drinks; sodium nitrite (E250) is used in cured meats. Without preservatives, many products would spoil within days of manufacture.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Colourings</strong> restore or enhance colour lost during processing, or make foods more visually appealing. Tartrazine (E102, a yellow dye) was historically used in squash and snacks. Some artificial colourings have been linked in research to hyperactivity in children, prompting manufacturers to switch to natural alternatives like beta-carotene (E160a, orange).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Flavour enhancers</strong> boost the existing flavour of a food without adding a distinct flavour of their own. Monosodium glutamate (E621) is the most widely used, found in savoury snacks and some ready meals. It amplifies the &ldquo;umami&rdquo; (savoury) taste receptor.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Emulsifiers</strong> keep oil and water mixed, preventing sauces and dressings from separating. Lecithin (E322, derived from soya or egg yolk) is used in chocolate to keep the cocoa butter and cocoa solids blended. Without emulsifiers, many processed products would split into unappetising layers within hours.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n24"><strong>Stabilisers</strong> maintain the texture and consistency of a food over time. Carrageenan (E407, extracted from seaweed) is used in dairy desserts and some infant formula. Guar gum (E412) is used in ice cream to prevent ice crystals forming on storage.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n25" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name one additive from each of the five categories (preservative, colouring, flavour enhancer, emulsifier, stabiliser) plus its E number and one food product it appears in.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>All UK-approved food additives carry an E number confirming a safety assessment. The five main functions are: preserving shelf life (e.g. sodium benzoate E211), adding or restoring colour (e.g. beta-carotene E160a), enhancing flavour (e.g. MSG E621), emulsifying oil and water (e.g. lecithin E322), and stabilising texture over time (e.g. carrageenan E407).</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n27">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n28">Primary processing converts raw agricultural materials into clean, storable ingredients (e.g. milling wheat to flour, pasteurising milk). Secondary processing uses primary ingredients to create new food products (e.g. bread, cheese, jam) &mdash; changing both sensory properties and nutritional value.</li><li data-narration-id="n29">Fortification adds nutrients to improve public health (e.g. calcium and iron added to white flour); modified foods are altered biologically or chemically (e.g. GM crops, lactose-free milk). Both have positive health and food security benefits alongside ethical and consumer-acceptance challenges.</li><li data-narration-id="n30">Additives each serve a specific function: preservatives extend shelf life, colourings improve appearance, flavour enhancers boost taste, emulsifiers prevent separation, and stabilisers maintain texture. All UK-approved additives carry an E number confirming safety.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n26">The 8-mark &ldquo;Discuss / Analyse&rdquo; question type rewards multiple perspectives &mdash; not just a list of facts. For a question about the effects of food modification or additives, the command word &ldquo;Discuss&rdquo; means &ldquo;present, analyse and consider relevant points&rdquo;. A common mistake is to write three paragraphs about positive effects and then add one brief sentence about negatives at the end. To reach the top band, both sides must be developed with technical vocabulary (named additives with E numbers, named modification techniques, named nutritional changes) and each point must be connected to a real food context &mdash; for example, &ldquo;sodium benzoate (E211) extends the shelf life of soft drinks, reducing food waste, but its use has been associated with allergic reactions in sensitive individuals.&rdquo; Aim for at least two developed points on each side before writing a brief summary sentence.</p>

