 {
  "text": "Which one of the following is the most appropriate adaptation for a recipe intended for someone with coeliac disease?\nA) Replace full-fat milk with skimmed milk\nB) Replace white wheat flour with certified gluten-free rice flour\nC) Replace butter with a low-fat spread\nD) Replace caster sugar with a sweetener",
  "type": "1 mark — Multiple Choice",
  "marks": "B) Replace white wheat flour with certified gluten-free rice flour [1 mark]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Name two of the four levers of recipe development.",
  "type": "1 mark — Identify / Name",
  "marks": "1 mark for any two of the following (both must be stated for the mark): ingredients; processes; cooking methods; portion sizes. [1 mark for two correct levers]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Describe two nutritional adaptations a food developer could make to a standard beef lasagne to make it more suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes.",
  "type": "3 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "Up to 3 marks: identification of a change (1), nutritional justification (1), second change (1). Acceptable answers: replace white pasta sheets with wholemeal pasta to increase NSP / slow glucose absorption (1 + 1); reduce the quantity of pasta per portion to lower the glycaemic load per serving (1 + 1); replace minced beef with lentils to add fibre and reduce saturated fat (1 + 1); use a tomato-based sauce with no added sugar rather than a sweet-tasting ready sauce (1 + 1). Maximum 3 marks."
 },
 {
  "text": "A school food technician is adapting a standard shortcrust pastry recipe for a student with coeliac disease. Explain why simply replacing wheat flour with a gluten-free alternative might not produce the same result, and what process change could help.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "1 mark each for up to 4 mark-earning points. Acceptable answers: gluten provides the stretchy network that holds pastry together and gives it structure when baked (1); removing it means the pastry is likely to be crumbly and difficult to roll without tearing (1); adding xanthan gum (a stabiliser) to the gluten-free flour blend helps replicate the binding function of gluten (1); chilling the pastry dough before rolling (process change) reduces handling time, which also helps hold the structure because the fat is firmer (1); using an extra egg yolk increases the fat and emulsifier content, improving binding (1). Maximum 4 marks."
 },
 {
  "text": "Nia is a 42-year-old working parent who arrives home at 7 pm after picking up her children. She wants to cook nutritionally balanced evening meals in under 30 minutes. Currently she relies on ready meals three evenings a week. Identify two ingredients, one process change and one technique change that could help her meet her brief, and explain how each contributes to the time or nutritional goal.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "1 mark each for up to 4 mark-earning points. Acceptable answers. Ingredients: pre-washed salad leaves or pre-cut stir-fry vegetables (1) save preparation time so more of the 30 minutes is spent cooking rather than chopping (1); tinned pulses such as chickpeas or lentils (1) require no soaking or long cooking, adding protein and NSP to a dish in under 5 minutes (1). Process: batch cooking a base sauce at the weekend (1) means a home-made tomato or curry sauce is available in the freezer and only needs reheating with fresh protein and vegetables (1). Technique: stir-frying in a hot wok (1) cooks lean protein and vegetables in under 10 minutes, producing a balanced meal with less fat than shallow-frying and with vegetables retaining more vitamins than boiling would allow (1). Maximum 4 marks."
 },
 {
  "text": "Theo, aged 16, has recently been diagnosed as lactose intolerant. His favourite family meal is a creamy chicken and mushroom pasta bake, made with double cream, butter and a Parmesan topping. His mother asks for help adapting the recipe so that Theo can still enjoy it.\n\nEvaluate how well a set of dairy-free adaptations — replacing double cream with oat cream, butter with a dairy-free spread, and Parmesan with a vegan Italian-style hard cheese — would meet Theo’s dietary need and maintain the sensory quality of the original dish. In your answer, consider the nutritional impact, sensory consequences, and any further adaptations that might improve the result. Reach a supported judgement about the overall success of the recipe development.",
  "type": "12 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Use StudyVault rubric.\n\nMastering (10-12 marks): comprehensive evaluation of all three adaptations across nutritional impact, sensory consequences and further improvements; accurate technical vocabulary (lactose, dairy-free alternatives, sensory descriptors, NSP, saturated fat); all three levers of the adaptation clearly connected to Theo's specific need; at least one further adaptation suggested with justification; a supported final judgement that weighs overall success rather than just listing changes.\n\nSecure (7-9 marks): covers most of the three adaptations with development; nutritional and sensory consequences addressed for most changes; a judgement is present but may not fully weigh strengths against weaknesses.\n\nDeveloping (4-6 marks): relevant points on adaptations but limited development; may cover nutritional or sensory but not both consistently; judgement present but weak or partially supported.\n\nEmerging (1-3 marks): basic identification of changes with little explanation of why; no or very limited sensory or nutritional analysis; no supported judgement.\n\nContent guidance: \nNutritional: replacing double cream with oat cream removes lactose (benefit for Theo's intolerance); oat cream is lower in saturated fat than dairy double cream (additional nutritional benefit); butter replacement removes a further lactose source; vegan hard cheese typically has less protein and less calcium than Parmesan (nutritional trade-off worth noting). \nSensory: oat cream has a thinner consistency and a slightly sweet, oat flavour compared to the rich, neutral creaminess of double cream — the sauce may be less thick and may have a perceptible different flavour; a process fix is to reduce the sauce for longer or add a cornflour slurry to improve viscosity; dairy-free spread behaves similarly to butter in a roux but may have a higher water content, making the sauce slightly thinner initially; vegan Italian-style hard cheese melts differently and may not form the same golden crust under the grill. \nFurther adaptations: adding a cornflour slurry to thicken the oat cream sauce; using a stronger-flavoured vegan cheese or adding a small amount of nutritional yeast to increase umami/cheesy depth; marinating the chicken in lemon juice and herbs before cooking to compensate for the reduced fat-richness of the sauce. \nJudgement: overall, the three adaptations successfully remove the key lactose sources from the dish (strong because the substitute ingredients are widely available and affordable), but the sensory quality — particularly the richness and creaminess — is likely to be noticeably different without further process changes to thicken the sauce. The adaptation is a good starting point but is not yet a direct equivalent of the original; Theo may need time to adjust his expectations of the dish's texture."
 }
